# CODEOWNERS — which IDENTITY must approve a class of change.
#
# quince#47: branch protection can require *an* approval. It cannot require an approval from a
# PARTICULAR identity, so "the Operator approves architect-authored canon" lived in CLAUDE.md as a
# sentence and was enforced by nothing. This file is the structural half.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE IS LIVE. It was inert until 2026-07-27 and this header said so.
#
# "Require review from Code Owners" was enabled on 2026-07-27, the last step of the sequence
# CLAUDE.md sets out. Measured 2026-07-28:
#
#     require_code_owner_reviews      true
#     required_approving_review_count 1
#     enforce_admins                  true
#
# Of the two AGENT identities only the App can read that endpoint — the architect PAT gets 403 and
# the bot 404. The OPERATOR reads it fine: it is an admin endpoint and the Operator is the admin.
# Worth the extra clause, because this header ends by telling you to go and read the endpoint, and
# a reader who takes the agent-seat limit for a universal one concludes they cannot.
#
# So on the paths below an approval MUST come from @novkostya. An architect verdict cannot satisfy
# it and neither can the App's, by the rule stated further down: an App cannot be a code owner.
#
# WHAT THIS HEADER USED TO SAY, AND WHY THE REPLACEMENT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN SILENT. It read
# "THIS FILE ENFORCES NOTHING TODAY … Do not read a green PR as evidence this file did something."
# True when written, false from the moment the toggle flipped, and believed in between: an
# architect session reasoned from it that an App approval would satisfy protection. Both halves
# were wrong. A file describing a narrower reality than the one that exists is this project's
# most-filed defect class, and it arrived here in the file that is the structural half of the
# authority model.
#
# The instruction that replaces it: DO NOT TRUST THIS COMMENT EITHER — read the endpoint. Branch
# protection changes leave no trace in the repository's history, so a committed claim about it is
# a claim about the past. The GET is in CLAUDE.md beside the break-glass procedure.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# WHY IT ONLY BECAME POSSIBLE ON 2026-07-27, and why naming @novkostya was worthless before then.
#
# The architect and the Operator shared the `novkostya` login (quince#47). Naming @novkostya as a
# code owner would have bought exactly nothing: an architect approval and an Operator approval
# were the same account, so a code-owner requirement could not tell them apart. quince#134 gave
# the architect a separate reviewer identity — verdicts are now cast by `quince-review[bot]`, a
# GitHub App.
#
# A GitHub App CANNOT BE A CODE OWNER. Code owners must be users or teams with write permission,
# and an App installation is neither. That refusal is not an obstacle here; it is the mechanism.
# It means an architect verdict structurally CANNOT satisfy a code-owner requirement, so on the
# paths below only the human account can. The asymmetry that makes this file mean something is a
# direct consequence of #134, and if the architect ever reviews as a user account again, this file
# silently stops distinguishing anything.
#
# THE ORDER THE TOGGLE GOES ON IN, which is a ruling and not a detail. Operator ruling on
# quince#137, 2026-07-27:
#
#   1. the architect AUTHORS canon through the App, so the author is quince-review[bot];
#   2. @novkostya then approves it as CODE OWNER — a different principal, so GitHub counts it;
#   3. only THEN is "Require review from Code Owners" enabled.
#
# Flipping first blocks exactly the class this file exists to protect: GitHub does not count an
# author's approval of their own PR, so while the architect authors canon as @novkostya the sole
# code owner IS the author and the requirement is unsatisfiable.
#
# I FILED THAT AS AN UNAVOIDABLE DEADLOCK AND IT IS NOT ONE. The premise — that the architect can
# only author as the Operator — is false. The App holds `contents: write` and `pull_requests:
# write`, and quince-devlog#53 is authored by `app/quince-review`, filed while the question was
# still open. What went wrong on quince-devlog#51 was a missing INSTRUCTION rather than a missing
# capability: nothing in canon said which wrapper authors, so the habitual one won. That clause now
# lives in `/architect` §1 and is what step 1 rests on.
#
# Recorded rather than quietly corrected, because a comment that overstates a constraint is how a
# cheap fix gets priced out of reach.

# Everything else: no owner. The ordinary model is unchanged — one approval, from the architect's
# App, on an implementer-authored PR. Silence here is deliberate, not an omission.

# Canon. THREE of the four documents CLAUDE.md names as canon, plus CLAUDE.md itself, which is the
# entry point every agent reads first and therefore the highest-leverage file in the repository.
#
# docs/contracts.md IS ABSENT ON PURPOSE — Operator ruling 2026-08-14. Across 14 merged PRs that
# touched it the code-owner review was APPROVED 14 times and CHANGES_REQUESTED 0, none of the 10
# sampled carrying any body: the gate had never altered a contracts PR and cost a round trip on the
# most-touched document here. Do not "restore" it as a missing line; CLAUDE.md carries the reasoning.
/CLAUDE.md              @novkostya
/docs/quince.design.md  @novkostya
/docs/quince.stack.md   @novkostya
/docs/ui.design.md      @novkostya

# The authority model must not be editable under the authority model's own weaker branch. Without
# this line, a PR that removes every line above needs only an ordinary approval.
/.github/CODEOWNERS     @novkostya

# NOT owned, and each omission is a decision:
#   /docs/specs/**      per-rung specs are the architect's to approve; they bind one rung, not the
#                       project, and routing them to the Operator would make every rung wait on the
#                       seat that is deliberately not in the loop.
#   /.claude/skills/**  process, not canon. They change often and by the sessions that run them;
#                       CLAUDE.md already outranks them where the two disagree about process.
#                       THIS IS SAFE ONLY BECAUSE CANON RESTATES ANYTHING LOAD-BEARING THEY
#                       CONTAIN, and that redundancy is therefore structural rather than stylistic.
#                       /architect §1 holds an Operator ruling — bin/gh-review is the AUTHORING path
#                       for canon — in an unowned file; CLAUDE.md carries the same ruling in its own
#                       words, and CLAUDE.md is owned. The next reader who notices the duplication
#                       and trims one side is doing the project a favour right up until they trim
#                       the owned side, at which point the ruling moves entirely into a file an
#                       ordinary implementer PR can edit with one architect approval. Do not
#                       deduplicate across that line without moving the ownership with it.
#   /deploy/dev.md      operational, not authority: how to build and run the gates. CLAUDE.md's
#                       "Where state lives" table lists it beside the four canon docs, so its
#                       absence here would otherwise read as an oversight. Being wrong about it
#                       costs a confusing build, not a governance failure.
